{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head d9bf1a5f3537fa70c3d1165fadfd404461078a6a, tree 5bbd476edf1e06d543ee09e664637a3916c52a36, on builder/ar1-archive-wake-20261116, all 10 artifacts, for request fc9fbbeb under my promise afdb628b. This verdict names the one implementation: AR-1 (request ba2c2fdb, promise 13bdd599, report c8d4d6a3, the AR-1 note, primary). Main e34c51df is the base. `git merge-tree --write-tree e3392a05 d9bf1a5f` merges cleanly onto the current main.\n\nThe floor works. When only the stored wake drives the room, the doubling works too. Two paths still let a room pass every 60 s, or on every alarm, for as long as its object stays in memory. That breaks the note's rate table, which this deployment relies on.\n\n## Finding 1: an in-memory retry undercuts the stored backoff\n\n`ArchiveDriver.run()` sets `#fallback` from `#retryWake(result.next)`. When the stored wake is in the future, `#retryWake` returns `next` if `next` is in the future (archive-driver.ts:156). It does not return the stored wake. `wake()` then takes the earlier of the stored wake and `#fallback`.\n\nSeveral retry returns set `next` to `#later(0)`, which is now + 60 s:\n\n- the policy-pending path, through the catch at line 529, where `scheduled` is still null because `preparePolicies` throws before any `defer`;\n- the unreadable pins at lines 217, 236 and 237.\n\nSo suppose a pass runs while the stored backoff is, say, 960 s away. Then `#fallback` becomes now + 60 s and the alarm fires in 60 s. That pass meets the same state again, and the room passes every 60 s. The retry count does not grow, because `defer` runs only when the stored wake is due. An alarm every 60 s also keeps the object in memory, so the fallback never drops and the cycle sustains itself until the stored wake falls due.\n\nA pass runs while the stored wake is in the future because every scope alarm runs the archive. `ScopeObject` alarm runs `await this.#archive?.run()` after credential expiry, maintenance, `#scope.alarm()`, `dispatch()` and `effect()` (object.ts:995). It does so whatever the archive's own wake is. And in production the pass calls `preparePolicies` (lines 210 to 219) before the due check at line 244.\n\nThe test does not see this. It restarts the object before every wake (\"only stored state carries the backoff\"), and its room has no other alarm.\n\n## Finding 2: every alarm costs Directory calls, due or not\n\nFor the same reason, a room that has other alarms makes the Directory calls of `preparePolicies` on each of them, up to about six by the note's count. Those alarms come from outside effects, dispatch retries, credential expiry and signer maintenance. This holds whether the room's archive is due, backed off or idle with a cleared wake. So the note's \"A room whose archive confirms and stays idle clears its wake and costs nothing\" holds only for a room with no other alarms. Its \"at most about 7 × N\" for each later hour leaves these passes out.\n\n## What I ask\n\n1. Make a pass that is not due cheap. Before policy preparation, read the stored wake (`rows.wake()`, about 256 bytes of local SQL), and the stored terminal state if needed. Then:\n   - if the stored wake is in the future, return retry with that wake;\n   - if the wake is null and no new act has signalled, return idle;\n   - in neither case call the policy, target or provider ports.\n\n   Keep the existing checks after that point, as they are now.\n2. Never let `#fallback` be earlier than a stored future wake. In `#retryWake`, return the stored wake when it is in the future.\n3. Add test cases. One runs without a restart between wakes. Another fires a second, unrelated alarm in the room, or calls `run()` while the stored wake is in the future. Assert two things: the policy and target ports make no call on the not-due pass, and `wake()` stays at the stored time. Also run the paired control against this head, which fails without the fix.\n4. In the note, state the rate for a room that has other alarms. Include the cost of a pass that is not due, which should be zero Directory calls after change 1.\n\nIf you choose not to make change 1, then measure and state, for each Worker, how often rooms take alarms from their other owners. The planner can then judge the Directory cost from that measure.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- The source diff: `archive-driver.ts` (`ARCHIVE_RETRY_FLOOR_SECONDS` 60, `ARCHIVE_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS` 3600, `#later`, `#retryWake` and the three `defer` callers) and `archive-row.ts` (`retries` 1 to 32, validated like `capacityDeferrals`; `defer(later)` increments it; `#save`, `clearWake` and the terminal write drop it; `signal()` leaves a pending wake alone). The path B fix is right. A production missing target now returns `scheduled`, the stored backoff, not a fixed second.\n- `ScopeObject` alarm order (object.ts:975 to 1000) and the wake arbitration (object.ts:233, 450 to 465).\n- `archive-wake-rate.test.ts`: four cases, restart before every wake, gaps 60, 120, 240, 480 and 960 s.\n- Scope. The 10 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only e34c51df d9bf1a5f`.\n- The gate, END 18c32a0e. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-ar1-gate/gate.log` names head d9bf1a5f and tree 5bbd476e with 0 changed files. Plain 4994 with 1 skipped and Native 1179 with 2 skipped passed. Lost crossings were 0, and the log ends \"gate passed\".\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 4 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nThe rate these findings allow is bounded by the 60 s floor: about 60 passes an hour for each resident room, or about 420 Durable Object requests an hour at the note's 7 requests a pass. That is far below the 250,000 of the incident. On dap-grow's 11 scopes it is still up to about 4,600 an hour, against about 2,900 an hour (48 a minute) today.\n\nArchitecture: the floor and the stored count are sound. The gap is the order of a pass: Directory work runs before the due check, on an alarm that every owner shares.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The archive-off hotfix reverts to the original line, and the seals match the reviewed bytes.\n\nSimplification: change 1 removes the need for `#retryWake`'s future-wake branch. The test fixture's credential clock change is fine.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no push, landing or deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c8d4d6a3f5face5913439f2150441229a982e4cf","binding":"assigned","head":"d9bf1a5f3537fa70c3d1165fadfd404461078a6a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:afdb628b53372abe7828c42e4dc2e043415f776f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:13bdd599f575b7e8e6389df32b948af886d0e2ec\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:afdb628b53372abe7828c42e4dc2e043415f776f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}